From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: ntdll: starting a Unix program leaks neither a handle nor a descriptor

NtCreateUserProcess, when the image was not a PE file and fork_and_exec
started it as a Unix program, returned at once and skipped its cleanup: the
image file's handle (from get_pe_file_info) and the working directory's
descriptor (get_unix_curdir) stayed open in the caller, one of each at every
start. Explorer runs sg-lockctl at every taskbar tick (the Linux windows'
buttons) and held about 20,000 handles after a day in the QA VM.

Gate: make test-unixexec.
--- a/dlls/ntdll/unix/process.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/unix/process.c
@@ -808,6 +808,12 @@
     {
         if (status == STATUS_INVALID_IMAGE_NOT_MZ && !fork_and_exec( &attr, unixdir, params ))
         {
+            /* Stained Glass OS: a Unix program was started: the image's
+             * handle and the working directory's descriptor are done with here
+             * too. Both leaked at every start -- explorer runs a Unix helper
+             * at every taskbar tick, and held 20,000 handles after a day. */
+            if (file_handle) NtClose( file_handle );
+            if (unixdir != -1) close( unixdir );
             memset( info, 0, sizeof(*info) );
             free( redir.Buffer );
             return STATUS_SUCCESS;
